 "cross_check_review": {
  "checked": "2026-09-25",
  "summary": "Stooq's closes add back dividends differently from the snapshot's, so the two differ on ex-dividend days: the third Friday of each quarter for SPY, XLF and XLE, June and December for EEM, 30 to 150 bps a time, each a step that stays. Stooq alone is wrong from 2015-03-12 to 2015-03-16, in every file: the snapshot's 23 funds agree with one another and SPY's -0.61% on 2015-03-13 is the S&P 500's. The other flagged days are single closes off by 10 to 80 bps in one source, reversed the next day. XLF, XLE and SLV are confirmed; SPY and EEM fail only the 2% bound on one day each, below.",
  "tickers": {
   "SPY": "201 bps on 2015-03-13 is Stooq's error above. The one real fault is the snapshot's: its close of 2009-07-16 is 1.1% too low (-0.16% that day while every other US fund of the snapshot rose 0.6% to 1.8%, then +1.10% the next day while the market was flat); the ratio of the two sources returns to its level on 2009-07-17. Returns over two days or more are unchanged; a decision on a weekly Friday close or a month-end close never reads that Thursday. The frozen snapshot is not edited: SPY is used as it is, with this day known.",
   "EEM": "One of the two sources has 2008-10-28 wrong by 3.7% (+20.9% in the snapshot, +25.6% at Stooq, whose close that day equals its high), reversed on 2008-10-29: the two-day return agrees within 3 bps, and the ratio returns to its level. Which source is right is not settled; either way the day is a large move in both, as the integrity review says. A decision on a weekly Friday close or a month-end close never reads that Tuesday; a daily volatility estimate over a window that holds it sees one day 3.7% off. The other flagged days are dividend days, Stooq's March 2015, and small reversed closes in 2007. EEM is used as it is, with this day known."
  }
 },
